I've made 943 serious mistakes in my life. I'm documenting every one of them so you don't have to make the same ones. My name is Lou Zerr. Here's another 4 of 943 (part 3 of 7).\par\par\par
The Friday I Opened The App And Looked At The Wrong Thing\par\par\par
I finally opened the sportsbook Friday night, and I still didn't see the line move.\par\par\par
I finally opened the sportsbook Friday night, and I still didn't see the line move.\par\par\par
This is the part of the story that embarrasses me most, because I can't blame it on not looking. I looked. I looked and I didn't see. Which is a whole other, worse kind of failure.\par\par\par
It was around 9. My wife was watching one of her shows, the one with the British detective who's always sad about something. I was on the couch pretending to read a book about the Roman Empire that a coworker had lent me two years ago. I had not made it past page 40. I was rereading the same paragraph about grain shipments and thinking about the bet.\par\par\par
I opened the app.\par\par\par
Here's what I did in the app. Follow me carefully because this is the exact geometry of the mistake. I opened the app. I tapped "My Bets." I saw my bet. Home team minus 3. There was my stake, three hundred, and the potential payout, which I had memorized. Green text where it needed to be green. Everything looked fine.\par\par\par
I closed the app.\par\par\par
Do you see it? I looked at my bet. I did not look at the line.\par\par\par
Those are two different acts. My bet was locked in at minus 3 the moment I placed it Tuesday. That number was never going to change on my screen. Home team minus 3, forever, until the game was played. My screen was a museum. It showed me what I had bought, not what the world had done to it since.\par\par\par
To see the line move I would have had to do one more thing. Back out of "My Bets." Tap the sport. Tap the game. Look at the live number. Compare. That's it. Three taps.\par\par\par
I didn't do the three taps.\par\par\par
And here's the honest reason, the reason I've been sitting with for weeks now. I didn't want to know. Somewhere underneath the top layer of my brain, some older, smarter, sadder layer knew that if I checked the current line, I might find out something I didn't want to find out. I might find out I was wrong. I might find out that the version of me from Tuesday, the version who had done the spreadsheet and hit place with such confidence, was already looking foolish by Friday.\par\par\par
So I looked at the frozen number. The number that could still be right. The Schrödinger's number, in the sealed box.\par\par\par
This is a pattern I have in my life. I check my bank account balance without looking at the transactions. I check my weight without looking at the trend. I check my email inbox without opening the ones from people I owe things to. I look at the surface of the thing I'm afraid of, confirm the surface exists, and close the app.\par\par\par
My wife glanced over. She said, you okay. I said, yeah, fine. She said, you're not reading. I said, I am. She said, you've been on that page for an hour. I said, it's dense.\par\par\par
It was not dense. I had read the paragraph nine times.\par\par\par
Around 9:30 I opened the app again. Same thing. My Bets. Green. Close. Around 10:15, one more time. Same routine. Museum, museum, museum. I did this three separate times on Friday night. Three separate opportunities to do three more taps. I did zero more taps.\par\par\par
By Friday at midnight the line was at minus 1. My screen still said minus 3. I went to bed feeling like a man who had checked on his position and found it acceptable.\par\par\par
Saturday I mowed. Saturday I watched college football. Saturday I opened the app twice and did the same museum walk. Never once did I look at the current number.\par\par\par
By Saturday night the line had crossed zero. The home team, my team, was now a slight underdog. In the language of the market, everyone had spent the week moving off the side I was on. And I had spent the week refusing to see it while telling myself I was staying on top of my bet.\par\par\par
Sunday morning I opened the app to watch the game.\par\par\par
And this time, at the top of the screen, the current line was too big to miss.\par\par\par
Sunday morning. Plus 1. My team. Not minus 3. Plus 1. Let me tell you what my face did.\par\par\par
\emdash  Lou\par\par\par
